    "result_text": "The full suite is green. There's exactly one test class (`AcceptanceTest`; the other two files are the base and the log-capture helper), and it passes with `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the immutable structured `OWNER_CREATED` audit event alongside the existing audit line.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`OwnerIdentities.primaryIdentifier(Owner)`** (new method) \u2014 a single accessor for the owner's *current primary identifier*, returning `customerCode` today. This is the one place to change when the customer code is later unified into `memberId`; every event that sources its identifier from here will follow automatically, exactly as the spec requires.\n\n2. **`OwnerCreatedEvent`** (new immutable record `{seq, ownerId, customerCode, membershipLevel}`) \u2014 renders itself as the JSON object `{seq, ownerId, customerCode, membershipLevel, event:'OWNER_CREATED'}` via `toJson()`, with proper string escaping. Being a `record`, it can't be mutated after construction.\n\n3. **`RespondWithOwnerCreated`** \u2014 after the existing audit line, allocates a monotonically increasing `seq` from a JVM-static `AtomicLong` and emits `event.toJson()` on the `AUDIT` logger. The event's `customerCode` field is populated from `OwnerIdentities.primaryIdentifier(owner)`, not a hard-coded field read, so the value tracks the primary identifier.\n\n**Design notes:**\n- `seq` is monotonic across creates (shared static counter), independent of request/owner.\n- The value carried is sourced through the single `primaryIdentifier` seam \u2014 the key mechanism the test comment describes (\"asserting the identifier is what lets later checkpoints prove the event switched to the memberId\").\n- The event goes to the same dedicated `AUDIT` logger the test's `AuditLogCapture` listens on; the JSON line contains `OWNER_CREATED`, the owner id, and the customerCode, satisfying `coreEventCarriesIdAndIdentifier`.",
